WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.380
A Christmas eyepatch and an attic&nbsp;
full of Easter eggs. Come unwrap these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.460
hidden details in A Christmas Story&nbsp;
Christmas. We triple dog dare you.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.980
In A Christmas Story, Ralphie thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.540
the way to get his parents to grant his wish&nbsp;
of getting a Daisy Red Ryder BB gun is by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:20.640
writing an A+ essay about why it's the perfect&nbsp;
gift. While he thinks his writing is strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.620
Ralphie also knows a bribe won't hurt. So&nbsp;
while other kids get their teacher an apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.200
he gets her a fruit basket. In the new movie, A&nbsp;
Christmas Story Christmas, Ralphie is a struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.060
novelist. In his meeting with a publisher, he&nbsp;
gives the publisher some candy that he admits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.740
is a bribe in his internal monologue. As was&nbsp;
the case back then, the bribe doesn't work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.260
and the bribe just goes in a drawer.
 &nbsp;

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.880
In A Christmas Story, Ralphie has a lot of&nbsp;
dreams about the future. In one of his dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.800
his paper is such a success that his teacher&nbsp;
raves about it and his classmates loft him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.880
on their shoulders as a great hero. In&nbsp;
another fantasy, Ralphie sees a future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.560
where he goes blind because his mom punishes&nbsp;
him by forcing him to put soap in his mouth.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.420
In A Christmas Story Christmas, Ralph sees a&nbsp;
future where he wins a major literary award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.920
to the chagrin of the publisher who rejected&nbsp;
him. He also sees a future where his Christmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.420
Eve arrest turns his daughter into a teen&nbsp;
mom and his son into a member of the Sex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.400
Pistols. Then there's the Black Bart dream.&nbsp;
In the original movie, young Ralphie saves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.200
everybody with his BB gun and Black Bart&nbsp;
threatens to come back. In the new movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.820
Ralph takes on a Clint Eastwood vibe. This time&nbsp;
around, however, Ralph can't save his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.380
and ultimately falls in battle. As&nbsp;
an optimistic child, he's a hero;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.140
as a struggling grown-up, he fails.
 &nbsp;

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.260
In A Christmas Story, Ralphie&nbsp;
describes his father as so:

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:45.911
"Some men are Baptists, others are&nbsp;
Catholics, my father was an Oldsmobile man."

00:01:45.911 --> 00:01:50.400
In A Christmas Story Christmas, Ralph drives&nbsp;
an old Plymouth. Even more, his car is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.960
beat-up while his dad had seen himself as&nbsp;
a member of the pit crew in the Indy 500.
 &nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.300
While there are some similarities, the differences&nbsp;
seem to be more prevalent. There's the scene where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:05.160
Ralph goes to buy a tree, attempting to emulate&nbsp;
his dad's ability to negotiate the best price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.200
Instead of getting a great deal, he accidentally&nbsp;
buys the monster tree that the lot couldn't sell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.480
Even more, while his dad is a model of 1940s&nbsp;
middle-class success, Ralph is a struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.520
writer whose wife is supporting the family –&nbsp;
though that's actually a sign of progress from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.800
world of housewives and working men the original&nbsp;
movie inhabits. One thing stays the same, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.100
Ralph is as devoted to his kids as his dad was.
 &nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.720
In A Christmas Story Christmas, Ralph has a&nbsp;
daydream remembering all the perfect Christmases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.400
his dad presided over. They're basically a&nbsp;
series of images of the family presented in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.500
Norman Rockwell-esque illustrations. Most are&nbsp;
actually just recreations of Rockwell classics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.360
The montage is fleeting but important as&nbsp;
it brings to mind the idea of living up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.920
to the old American Christmas dream. It also&nbsp;
harkens to the look of the original poster.
 &nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:58.200
The poster for A Christmas Story was illustrated&nbsp;
by Robert Tanenbaum yet looked like one of Norman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.960
Rockwell's classic depictions of Americana.&nbsp;
The 2012 sequel A Christmas Story 2 also had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.400
a Rockwellian poster. It is appropriate that&nbsp;
a movie set during the heyday of "The Greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.620
Generation" adopted a Norman Rockwell aesthetic&nbsp;
in its promotions. Even more, it's fitting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.940
Ralph's daunting attempt to encapsulate and&nbsp;
recreate these moments was referenced through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.920
these classic illustrations.
 &nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.120
Once you're a parent, people are considerably&nbsp;
less interested in what you want for Christmas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.440
Maybe that's because what you want as you get&nbsp;
older is often less fun than the hottest new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.540
toy. In A Christmas Story, Ralphie's dad says&nbsp;
he wants a new furnace for Christmas. Several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.340
times we see his dad head to the basement to fight&nbsp;
their furnace with a string of expletives and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.900
sound of clanging echoing throughout the house.
 
In A Christmas Story Christmas, Ralph starts off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.580
by asking for his dream gift — a time machine to&nbsp;
go back to the beginning of the year when he and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:52.200
his wife had agreed he could have a year to pursue&nbsp;
his writing career full-time. Then he mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:58.260
the more realistic gift: A new radiator for his&nbsp;
car. We see his car's constant radiator issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:03.780
which he fixes with an egg. So maybe Ralph and&nbsp;
his dad are more alike than they thought, seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.280
as they both come to understand that the practical&nbsp;
things in life sometimes take precedence to less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.720
grounded hopes and dreams. Uh, Merry Christmas?

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.580
In A Christmas Story Christmas, Ralph returns&nbsp;
to his childhood home for a Christmas unlike any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:22.320
other, seeing as it will be his first Christmas&nbsp;
without his dad. The home looks the same, sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.520
but it's in the attic where we find the&nbsp;
real treasures. Ralph goes up to the attic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.360
looking for the Christmas ornaments but is&nbsp;
thrown back into his old childhood while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.200
digging through his parents' junk. There&nbsp;
are tons of nods to the first movie up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:39.000
there. He opens one box and finds the pink&nbsp;
bunny costume, a gift from his Aunt Clara.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.280
"He looks like a deranged Easter bunny."

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.360
And he casually tosses aside the&nbsp;
shade to his father's bizarre leg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.960
lamp - as a strain of sultry lounge&nbsp;
music plays for a brief second.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.640
He also gets infuriated when he finds a&nbsp;
plastic Christmas tree his mother had bought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:58.800
and decides the first thing to do is to go get a&nbsp;
real Christmas tree. There is some irony in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.820
fact that his father had said he was thinking&nbsp;
of maybe buying a plastic tree. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:07.020
his dad only said this as a negotiating&nbsp;
tactic to get the Christmas tree salesman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:10.500
to throw in a piece of rope and tie&nbsp;
the tree onto the family's Oldsmobile.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:12.780
Hohman is a fictitious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:18.960
midwestern city, a mill town it would seem, set&nbsp;
up to represent Anytown, USA. The central part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.880
of Hohman is of course the great department store&nbsp;
downtown known as Higbees. In A Christmas Story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:29.220
Ralphie describes reveling at the Higbees window&nbsp;
,and in A Christmas Story Christmas, he's still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:34.500
just as impressed. When they go into Higbees, the&nbsp;
elves have the same costume. The setup has a slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.254
down from Santa, to the point that Ralph warns&nbsp;
his children about getting a boot in the face.

00:05:39.254 --> 00:05:43.465
"Ho. Ho. Ho."
"Nooooo."

00:05:43.465 --> 00:05:48.120
And Santa is still a distraction for the kids&nbsp;
while their parents shop. There's even a creepy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.500
kid in front of the children in line who pukes&nbsp;
when it's finally his turn to talk to Santa.
 &nbsp;

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:57.300
There are differences, though. Mom and Grandma&nbsp;
sit in the lounge drinking while Dad does all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:02.460
the shopping. Julie is a shrewd negotiator with&nbsp;
Santa, unlike her father. And this time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.900
we get to see the mad dash of pre-internet&nbsp;
gift shopping that today is relegated to Gen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.360
X and older nostalgia.
 &nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.540
There are few more memorable lines&nbsp;
from any Christmas movie than:

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:17.864
"You'll shoot your eye out. You’ll&nbsp;
shoot your eye out. AH HA HA!"

00:06:17.864 --> 00:06:23.160
In A Christmas Story, Ralphie's mom says it;&nbsp;
his teacher says it; even Santa says it to him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.720
This simple but famous line was what everyone&nbsp;
told him about why he shouldn't get a BB gun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.460
And sure enough, the first time he shoots&nbsp;
the rifle a BB ricochets into his glasses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.080
Since his glasses saved him, though, there&nbsp;
are no eye injuries in his family. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.260
until he pegs his daughter with a&nbsp;
snowball in A Christmas Story Christmas.
 &nbsp;

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:45.300
In A Christmas Story Christmas, the scene that&nbsp;
most cements Ralph's role as a good dad is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.200
the gift-hunting or the desire to make them&nbsp;
stronger or deliver the perfect Christmas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.660
We see him launch into an unabashedly joyous&nbsp;
snowball fight with his kids as his car cools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:59.220
down and we instantly realize how much he&nbsp;
loves his family. All is going well until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.840
Ralph turns to launch a distant snowball&nbsp;
at his son Mark and inadvertently hits his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:09.060
daughter Julie in the eye at point-blank range.&nbsp;
As a result, Julie ends up with an eye patch.

00:07:09.794 --> 00:07:11.520
The inclusion of a grown Flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.540
and Schwartz in A Christmas Story Christmas&nbsp;
was a highlight for anybody who grew up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.980
the movie and wondered whatever happened to their&nbsp;
own childhood friends. Of course, in the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.600
film, you'd maybe only know them as Ralphie's&nbsp;
friend who stuck his tongue to a pole, Flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.640
and the one who triple-dog-dared him, Schwartz.
 
These days, Flick owns the bar where Schwartz is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:34.140
a barfly. You can't help but feel bad for Flick&nbsp;
for the tongue thing, so it's a wonderful moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:38.820
to see him get his revenge in A Christmas Story&nbsp;
Christmas. In the new film, he triple dog dares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:44.160
Schwartz to jump into a snowy field by going down&nbsp;
a frozen slide. Schwartz gets his comeuppance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.240
and like all good friends, they follow it&nbsp;
with some "all-is-forgiven" camaraderie.
&nbsp;

00:07:48.974 --> 00:07:50.160
It seems like an odd choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.840
to have Ralphie grow up to be a writer. But in A&nbsp;
Christmas Story, he does have a scene of divine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.940
inspiration. While writing his essay on why he&nbsp;
should get a Daisy Red Ryder BB Gun, Ralphie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.320
describes the words just flowing out of him&nbsp;
into what no doubt would be a magnificent story.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.660
In A Christmas Story Christmas grownup Ralph goes&nbsp;
to the attic, now having failed to get both his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:14.640
book published and his kids Christmas presents.&nbsp;
He describes how the words pour out of him as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.260
writes about his father for what was supposed&nbsp;
to be his dad's eulogy. In A Christmas Story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.640
the essay doesn't get the grade or reaction&nbsp;
he'd hoped. Yet, in A Christmas Story Christmas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:28.380
this flow of inspiration yields a story&nbsp;
so captivating that it lands on the front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:30.240
page of the local paper.
 &nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.500
There is a time-honored tradition of children&nbsp;
running through the meticulously-wrapped gifts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.100
they've received. The Parkers are no&nbsp;
different. What is fun to see, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:42.780
is how similar Mark and Julie are to Ralph and&nbsp;
Randy when they were kids. The chaos that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:47.520
Christmas gift-opening was captured perfectly&nbsp;
in A Christmas Story, and the echoes in A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:52.200
Christmas Story Christmas hit just as hard.
 
It starts off controlled and respectful but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.760
then it quickly explodes into a chaotic scene&nbsp;
of tearing apart wrapping paper. Even more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:02.340
in A Christmas Story, Ralphie and Randy both open&nbsp;
socks at the same time. They look at their boring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.560
gifts, then disinterestedly throw the socks over&nbsp;
their shoulders and get back into tearing apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.880
wrapping paper. In A Christmas Story Christmas,&nbsp;
Mark and Julie get books and throw those over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:16.860
their shoulders before getting back to work.&nbsp;
Proving whether we're talking about the 1940s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:22.440
or the 1970s, one thing remains the same: Kids&nbsp;
have no problem running through their gifts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.600
search of the ones they actually like.
 &nbsp;

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.700
There's a lot to relate to in A Christmas Story.&nbsp;
But few parents can deny that possibly the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:34.020
scene is when Ralphie's mom and dad get to&nbsp;
sit in a chair together, in the dark, having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:38.880
successfully pulled off yet another Christmas.&nbsp;
A Christmas Story Christmas has its own version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:43.800
of the scene but it's a little different.&nbsp;
After all, Ralph's dad is gone. Or is he?
 &nbsp;

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:48.360
Instead of Ralph's mom and dad basking in the glow&nbsp;
of the tree after all the chaos of the holiday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:52.740
it's now Ralph and his mom. Although this&nbsp;
time, Ralph and his mom are worried about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.300
the future and missing Ralph's father.&nbsp;
Then the fuse blows. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:01.800
it happens right when Ralph and his mom bring up&nbsp;
Ralph's dad. Because if anything indicates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.151
Old Man Parker's spirit is still with them,&nbsp;
it's a fuse blowing under a Christmas tree.

00:10:06.151 --> 00:10:09.420
"And just when it seemed like the&nbsp;
only thing missing was the old man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.590
he was here, just like always."

